module lib/hf/casida  documents every variable
  Excitation energies of a closed-shell molecule from its Hartree-Fock
  ground state, by linear response: the Casida equation.

  A molecule absorbs light at the energies of its excited states above the
  ground state, its excitation energies. Hartree-Fock gives only the ground
  state: a set of doubly occupied orbitals. Linear response asks how that
  state responds to a weak field oscillating at frequency $\omega$; the
  response diverges at the $\omega$ that are excitation energies, and those
  solve an eigenvalue problem in the space of occupied-virtual pairs $ia$
  (an electron moved from occupied orbital $i$ to virtual orbital $a$).

  Two matrices over those pairs describe it. $A$ couples one excitation to
  another: the orbital energy difference $\varepsilon_a - \varepsilon_i$ on
  its diagonal, plus the change of the electrons' repulsion. $B$ couples an
  excitation to a de-excitation (an electron moved back). Keeping $B$ gives
  time-dependent Hartree-Fock (TDHF), also called the random-phase
  approximation; dropping it leaves $A$ alone, the Tamm-Dancoff
  approximation (TDA), which is configuration interaction with single
  excitations. Singlets and triplets (total spin 0 and 1) have different
  $A$ and $B$.

casida_mo  @e893a2f6
  how   The orbitals are the eigenvectors of the Fock matrix built from the
        converged density (lib/hf/stab's orbital pack). The Fock matrix in
        that orbital basis is diagonal, and its diagonal is the orbital
        energies. The two-electron integrals move from the atomic-orbital
        basis to the orbitals by four quarter transformations, one index
        at a time.
  why   The Casida matrices are written in the orbital basis: their entries
        are orbital energies and integrals over orbitals, not over the
        atomic basis functions the SCF works in.
  p     the SCF setup: $H$, $S$, the packed integrals, the electron counts
  r     the converged RHF result
  n     the number of basis functions
  orb   the orbital pack `[1, n + 1, m]`: the orbitals' coefficients on the
        basis functions, and a row of occupation flags
  m     the number of orbitals, occupied and virtual
  Fm    the Fock matrix in the orbital basis, `[1, m, m]`: diagonal, since
        the orbitals are its eigenvectors
  eps   $\varepsilon$  the orbital energies, lowest first, hartree
  q     an orbital
  M     $(pq|rs)$  the two-electron integrals over orbitals, `[m, m, m, m]`
  nocc  the number of doubly occupied orbitals

  orbitals   the orbitals: the eigenvectors of the Fock matrix of the converged density
  energies   the orbital energies: the diagonal of the Fock matrix in the orbital basis
    run        The orbital energies are {eps} hartree, lowest first; the first nocc are occupied.
  integrals  the two-electron integrals over orbitals, from the atomic-orbital ones by four quarter transformations
    $$ (pq|rs) = \sum_{\mu\nu\lambda\sigma} C_{\mu p} C_{\nu q} C_{\lambda r} C_{\sigma s}\, (\mu\nu|\lambda\sigma) $$

casida_ab  @9bc8110b
  how   One pass over every pair of pairs $(ia, jb)$. The pair $ia$ is
        stored at index `i * nvir + a`, with $a$ counted from the first
        virtual orbital.
  why   The triplet matrices lack the Coulomb term $2(ia|jb)$: a triplet
        excitation flips a spin, and a spin flip moves no net charge, so
        there is no change of the classical repulsion to add.
  eps    $\varepsilon$  the orbital energies
  M      $(pq|rs)$  the integrals over orbitals
  nocc   $n_o$  the number of occupied orbitals
  nvir   $n_v$  the number of virtual orbitals
  spin   `0` for singlets, `1` for triplets
  A      $A$  the excitation-excitation matrix, `[nov, nov]`
  B      $B$  the excitation-de-excitation matrix
  coul   $c$  the weight of the Coulomb term: `2` for singlets, `0` for triplets
  i      $i$  an occupied orbital
  a      $a$  a virtual orbital, counted from the first virtual one
  ia     the row: the pair $ia$
  j      $j$  an occupied orbital
  b      $b$  a virtual orbital, counted from the first virtual one
  jb     the column: the pair $jb$

  $$ A_{ia,jb} = \delta_{ij}\delta_{ab}(\varepsilon_a - \varepsilon_i) + c\,(ia|jb) - (ij|ab), \qquad B_{ia,jb} = c\,(ia|jb) - (ib|ja) $$  [hold "A is symmetric"]
  = A#2  the Coulomb term, the classical repulsion of the charge moved, minus the exchange term $(ij|ab)$
  = B#2  the same Coulomb term, minus the exchange term that couples to the de-excitation, $(ib|ja)$
  = A#3  on the diagonal, the orbital energy difference: the excitation energy if the electrons did not rearrange

casida_tda  @132f3c19
  why   TDA is the symmetric eigenvalue problem $A X = \omega X$: the
        excitations interact with each other and not with the
        de-excitations
        [test "for one occupied-virtual pair TDA gives A and TDHF gives sqrt(A^2 - B^2)"].
        eig_sym reads A and leaves it as it was, for TDHF.
  A  $A$  the excitation-excitation matrix
  n  the number of occupied-virtual pairs
  w  $\omega$  the eigenvalues, the TDA excitation energies
  V  the eigenvectors, the amplitudes $X$ (not used further)

casida_tdhf  @af654937
  how   The TDHF problem has size $2n$ and is not symmetric. Adding and
        subtracting its two block rows gives $(A + B)(X + Y) = \omega (X - Y)$
        and $(A - B)(X - Y) = \omega (X + Y)$, so
        $(A - B)(A + B)(X + Y) = \omega^2 (X + Y)$. With $R = (A - B)^{1/2}$
        and $Z = R^{-1}(X + Y)$ this is the symmetric problem
        $R (A + B) R\, Z = \omega^2 Z$ of size $n$.
  why   A symmetric problem has real eigenvalues and orthogonal
        eigenvectors, and a standard solver handles it
        [test "for one occupied-virtual pair TDA gives A and TDHF gives sqrt(A^2 - B^2)"].
        The square root needs $A - B$ positive definite; otherwise the
        energies come back as $-1$
        [test "an A - B that is not positive definite gives -1 in place of the TDHF energies"].
  A     $A$  the excitation-excitation matrix
  B     $B$  the excitation-de-excitation matrix
  n     the number of occupied-virtual pairs
  w     the eigenvalues of $A - B$
  V     the eigenvectors of $A - B$; later of the folded matrix
  wmin  the lowest eigenvalue of $A - B$
  Vs    the eigenvectors of $A - B$, each times the square root of its eigenvalue
  k     an eigenvector
  R     $(A - B)^{1/2}$  the square root of $A - B$
  C     $R (A + B) R$  the folded, symmetric matrix
  lam   $\omega^2$  its eigenvalues
  om    $\omega$  the TDHF excitation energies, ascending

  $$ \begin{pmatrix} A & B \\ -B & -A \end{pmatrix}\begin{pmatrix} X \\ Y \end{pmatrix} = \omega \begin{pmatrix} X \\ Y \end{pmatrix} \quad\Rightarrow\quad (A - B)^{1/2} (A + B) (A - B)^{1/2} Z = \omega^2 Z $$  [test "for one occupied-virtual pair TDA gives A and TDHF gives sqrt(A^2 - B^2)"]
  stability    the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of A - B, and the lowest of them
    why          $A - B$ positive definite means no rotation of the orbitals into complex ones lowers the energy: the ground state is stable against those rotations [hold "A - B is positive definite: the ground state is stable against these rotations"]. For a closed shell the Coulomb terms of $A$ and $B$ cancel in $A - B$, so singlets and triplets share it.
    run          The lowest eigenvalue of $A - B$ is {wmin} hartree.
  square_root  R, the square root of A - B, from its eigenvectors and the square roots of its eigenvalues
    $$ R = (A - B)^{1/2} = V\, \mathrm{diag}(\sqrt{w})\, V^\top $$
  fold         the symmetric matrix C = R (A + B) R
    $$ C = (A - B)^{1/2} (A + B) (A - B)^{1/2} $$  [hold "the folded matrix is symmetric"]
  solve        the eigenvalues of C are the squares of the TDHF energies: their square roots, ascending
  return the energies

casida_solve  @433df73f
  how   Build $A$ and $B$ for the spin kind, then solve the TDA problem with
        $A$ and the TDHF problem with both.
  why   The lowest TDHF energy is never above the lowest TDA energy. The
        lowest TDHF $\omega$ is the minimum of a ratio over vectors
        $(X, Y)$; the vectors with $Y = 0$ are among them, and over those
        the ratio's minimum is the lowest TDA energy
        [hold "the lowest TDHF energy is not above the lowest TDA energy"].
  mo           what the ground state gives: orbital energies, integrals over orbitals, and the occupied and virtual counts
  spin         CASIDA_SINGLET or CASIDA_TRIPLET
  nov          the number of occupied-virtual pairs, the size of $A$ and $B$
  A            $A$  the excitation-excitation matrix
  B            $B$  the excitation-de-excitation matrix
  tda          the TDA energies, ascending, hartree
  lowest_tda   the lowest TDA energy
  tdhf         the TDHF energies, ascending, hartree
  lowest_tdhf  the lowest TDHF energy

  build  A and B for this spin kind, from the orbital energies and the integrals
    run    Each is {nov} by {nov}: one row per occupied-virtual pair.
  tda    the TDA energies: the eigenvalues of A
    run    The lowest is {lowest_tda} hartree.
  tdhf   the TDHF energies, with B
    predict  $B$ couples each excitation to the de-excitations. Will the lowest energy move up or down from TDA's?
    run      Down: the lowest is {lowest_tdhf} hartree, against {lowest_tda} for TDA.
  return both lists
